WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.130
In the winter of 1911-1912, a political earthquake
in Asia brought one of our last links to the

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.750
ancient world crashing down.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.860
Imperial China had outlasted the Western Roman
Empire, the Byzantine Empire, the Holy Roman

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:14.860
Empire.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.130
For 2,000 years, it had withstood civil wars
and foreign invasion, only to dramatically

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:22.500
implode after less than six months of unrest.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.110
The emperor on the throne at this turning
point in history?

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.360
A six year old boy named Puyi.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:33.280
Born into an ossified court and brought up
in unimaginable luxury, the boy emperor spent

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.380
his first six years as a living god… before
finding himself turned into a political fossil,

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.269
a relic of a dead age.

00:00:41.269 --> 00:00:44.370
But there was more to Puyi than just one frightened
child.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.140
An entitled brat who grew up to be a spoiled
playboy before ending his life a convicted

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:55.250
war criminal, Puyi was the living embodiment
of China’s crazed 20th Century transformation.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:12.800
Join us today as we journey through the life
of China’s Last Emperor.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.260
The Politics of Power
On 7 February, 1906, the boy who would one

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.270
day rule China opened his eyes for the very
first time.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.710
At the moment of his birth, Puyi was perhaps
the single most privileged child on the face

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:23.710
of the planet.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:28.290
His father, Prince Chun, was a member of of
the Qing dynasty, a group of ethnic Manchus

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.200
who’d ruled China since 1644.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.630
His uncle, Guangxu, was the current emperor.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.340
Yet the apparent stability of Puyi’s young
life masked a much deeper series of problems.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:44.680
By 1906, the 2,000 year old Chinese empire
was less a Dàshà built on rock than it was

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.180
a Zhǐpái wū built atop shifting sands.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.710
Oh, and this slippery, treacherous sand had
a name.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.150
It called itself Cixi, the Dowager Empress
of China.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:58.030
Born in November, 1835, Cixi had once been
a low-ranking concubine of the Xianfeng Emperor.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:02.590
However, she’d also been the only person
to bear the emperor a son, so when old Xianfeng

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:07.890
died in 1861, Cixi had suddenly gone from
“ absolute nobody” to “mother of the

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:08.890
new emperor”.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:12.040
Not that “mother of the emperor” was a
job with many perks.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.170
While Cixi’s emperor-son Tongzhi was still
a child, all the ruling was done on his behalf

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.970
by an 8 member regent council.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:25.269
From the council’s perspective, Cixi was
a nuisance, a concubine who’d gotten lucky.

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:29.569
But while Cixi certainly had gotten lucky
with her pregnancy, she was also someone who

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:30.989
made her own luck.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:35.930
In November, 1861, Cixi orchestrated a palace
coup, kicking out the council and installing

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:39.489
herself and two loyalists as regents in their
place.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:44.090
It was an arrangement that effectively handed
Cixi the keys to China.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.790
So long as her emperor son was a minor, she
could rule on his behalf.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:52.329
And once he was no longer a minor, well…
there are always other children, right?

00:02:52.329 --> 00:02:59.110
On January 12, 1875, Tongzhi died aged 19
in circumstances so suspicious he might as

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.010
well have been literally suffocated by Cixi’s
ambition.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:06.180
Barely was the emperor’s body cold than
Cixi announced she’d adopted her five year

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.000
old nephew and made him the new Guangxu Emperor.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.620
Now, normally just declaring your adopted
nephew emperor of China would go about as

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.379
well as, well, you or I declaring our adopted
nephew emperor of China, but by 1875 Cixi

00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:22.840
had built such a powerbase that nobody could
stand in her way.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:28.620
And so came the second period of Chinese history
where a little boy play acted at being emperor

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.510
while Cixi did all the actual ruling.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.561
This time round, though, Cixi allowed the
emperor to reach maturity, presumably on the

00:03:34.561 --> 00:03:37.700
assumption that he’d just do whatever she
said anyway.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.329
But Cixi had badly misjudged Guangxu.

00:03:40.329 --> 00:03:44.499
In 1898, the emperor suddenly did something
that shocked her to her core.

00:03:44.499 --> 00:03:48.260
He grew a backbone and tried to reform Imperial
China.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.469
This was actually a very astute move.

00:03:50.469 --> 00:03:55.340
By 1898, the Qing dynasty had lost two opium
wars, barely survived the Taiping Rebellion,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.700
been humiliated in the First Sino-Japanese
War, and had almost lost their mandate as

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.049
Manchus to be ruling over the ethnic Han Chinese.

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:07.650
The only way to stop a revolution from exploding
was to introduce something like a European

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:08.650
system.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:12.049
But that would involve both Guangxu and Cixi
giving up power.

00:04:12.049 --> 00:04:18.440
And asking Cixi to give up power was like
asking Napoleon to go easy on all the conquering.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:24.360
On September 22, 1898, a military force loyal
to Cixi converged on the Forbidden City.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.910
Guangxu was imprisoned in his palace, his
reforms scrapped, and his supporters driven

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:30.910
into exile.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.199
In his place, Cixi assumed full control of
China.

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:39.380
And that’s where things stood when Puyi
was born in 1906, with the emperor a prisoner,

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:45.080
Cixi on the throne, and the public close to
rioting over Guangxu’s blocked reforms.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.640
Little did the newborn child know it, but
soon all those factors would combine to destroy

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.560
his entire world.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:59.340
The Last Emperor: Part I
It was a chilly mid-November day when Dowager

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:04.460
Empress Cixi breathed her last in one of the
vast and gloomy rooms of the imperial palace.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:08.699
Her penultimate act was to inform the world
that the Guangxu Emperor had died the day

00:05:08.699 --> 00:05:11.919
before - almost certainly poisoned on Cixi’s
orders.

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:16.200
Then, with her last breath already rattling
in her throat, she named her successor as

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.480
little Puyi.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.050
Cixi passed away on November 15, 1908.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:24.300
Barely two weeks later, two year old Puyi
was coronated in the heart of the Forbidden

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.300
City.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:28.740
It’s said that when baby Puyi first saw
the scale of the palace, heard the drums,

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:33.199
and saw the thousands of eunuchs there to
witness his coronation, he bawled his eyes

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.199
out.

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:38.319
For the toddler, that ceremony marked the
moment he forever lost any chance of a normal

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:39.319
childhood.

00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:42.870
As the new emperor, Puyi was now followed
everywhere by armies of eunuchs attending

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:43.940
his every whim.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:48.150
To give just one slightly gross example, every
time he did a poop he would be watched by

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:53.430
dozens of grown men, who would then regally
pass his bedpan to the head eunuch for examination.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.530
Still, there were upsides to being a living
God, especially if you were a tiny sadist.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:01.979
You know that old Twilight Zone episode where
everyone is super-scared of the little boy

00:06:01.979 --> 00:06:05.199
who can read minds and turn people into jack-in-the-boxes?

00:06:05.199 --> 00:06:07.030
That was basically Puyi’s childhood.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:11.379
At a young age, the boy emperor discovered
everyone had to do whatever he told them.

00:06:11.379 --> 00:06:16.030
And young Puyi loved nothing more than ordering
his servants to beat random eunuchs into a

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:17.540
wailing, bloody pulp.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.250
Not that Puyi would hold this power for long.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:25.789
On October 9, 1911, an explosion leveled a
small house in the central city of Wuchang,

00:06:25.789 --> 00:06:27.439
now part of Wuhan.

00:06:27.439 --> 00:06:31.129
When police investigated the blast, they found
in the rubble a list of names linked to a

00:06:31.129 --> 00:06:32.930
nationalist revolutionary movement.

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:37.290
At the time, China was suffering a constant,
low-level threat from such movements.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:41.539
Not that they ever went anywhere, which may
be why the police seemed so blase about the

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:42.539
list.

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:45.880
I mean, what were these dumb revolutionaries
gonna do?

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.139
Capture the whole of Wucha-... oh, wait a
minute.

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:51.220
That’s exactly what they did!

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:56.039
By December, the uprising in Wuchang had inspired
fourteen provinces to go into rebellion.

00:06:56.039 --> 00:07:02.610
A provisional government had been established
in Nanjing under arch revolutionary Sun Yat-sen.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:06.940
Seeing their reign on the verge of collapse,
Prince Chun called the great general Yuan

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:10.159
Shikai out of retirement to save the Qing
dynasty.

00:07:10.159 --> 00:07:16.110
Late that month, Yuan finally asked representatives
of the rebel government what they wanted.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:21.650
The end of the Qing, they told him.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:22.650
That’s it?

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:23.650
That’s it.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:28.919
In that case - and here we like to image Yuan
Shikai giving a sly little smile - allow me

00:07:28.919 --> 00:07:30.860
to help.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:38.050
God No More
When the end of Imperial China came, it was

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.000
with embarrassing swiftness.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.689
In January, the rebels declared a republic
centered around Nanjing.

00:07:43.689 --> 00:07:47.889
On the 26th of that month, Yuan went to the
palace and told the imperial family he was

00:07:47.889 --> 00:07:53.270
prepared for war, but that in his estimation
they would all end up as dead as a Norwegian

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:54.270
parrot.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.460
At first, some of the family held out.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.099
But Yuan was… persuasive.

00:07:58.099 --> 00:08:00.810
Working behind the scenes, he offered the
Qing a simple choice.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:05.389
Either accept a massive bribe now to end their
dynasty, or be beheaded when the rebels finally

00:08:05.389 --> 00:08:06.389
took Beijing.

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:08.120
Everyone took the bribe.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:12.150
On February 12, 1912, six year old Puyi was
forced to abdicate.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:16.001
The rebels had already agreed that he could
continue to live in the Forbidden City, even

00:08:16.001 --> 00:08:19.870
keep his servants and be given enough money
to live like an emperor for the rest of his

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:20.870
life.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:24.629
Given all this, it’s highly unlikely the
young boy even noticed anything had changed.

00:08:24.629 --> 00:08:27.449
Even noticed that his palace had now become
his prison.

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:29.919
That March, a new constitution was promulgated.

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:34.190
Yuan’s help in ending the Qing was rewarded
by the rebels, who made him president.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:39.120
And, just like that, 2,000 years of imperial
Chinese rule came to an abrupt end.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.159
Well, almost.

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:44.120
While Puyi may be known to history as the
Last Emperor, there was actually another emperor

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.649
who started his reign after Puyi abdicated.

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:49.209
You already know him as Yuan Shikai.

00:08:49.209 --> 00:08:54.620
And his imperial ambitions were about to ensure
China’s 20th Century would be a nightmare.

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:59.300
The problems started when the Chinese republic
held its first elections in January, 1913.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.839
As you might expect, they returned a majority
for the rebels.

00:09:01.839 --> 00:09:06.930
But President Yuan decided he no longer needed
these guys now the Qing were gone, and had

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.950
the head of the National Assembly murdered.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:13.360
This resulted in the rebels taking up arms
again, only for Yuan to really go to war this

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:14.360
time.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.350
He might have told the Qing in January, 1912
that a war could only end in their defeat,

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:20.480
but when it was his own power on the line,
he clearly felt differently.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.600
By March, 1913, he’d won.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.010
Now, Yuan had only won in a loose sense.

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:28.870
In many provinces, power was concentrated
in the hands of military leaders who only

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:30.370
paid lip service to Beijing.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:33.630
But, still, Yuan had no immediate challengers
to his rule.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:38.490
Come the end of 1915, he was even feeling
secure enough to have himself declared the

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.149
new Hongxian Emperor.

00:09:40.149 --> 00:09:41.840
This turned out to be a mistake.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.730
See, Yuan won the 1913 war because of the
backing of the military.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:49.450
But even the military wasn’t willing to
kowtow to an illegitimate emperor.

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:54.040
83 days after announcing his new empire, Yuan
was forced at gunpoint to dissolve it.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:59.040
Barely three months after that, on June 6,
1916, President Yuan died - by some measures

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.180
the true “last” emperor of China.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.720
But only by some measures.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.630
After Yuan died, China disintegrated into
multiple warring states ruled by former military

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:08.630
generals.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:12.360
In the second year of what became known to
history as the Warlord Era, a Qing loyalist

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:16.420
named Zhang Xun marched on Beijing, and restored
Puyi to the throne.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:21.029
By now a boy of 11, Puyi ruled during his
second reign for a mere eleven days before

00:10:21.029 --> 00:10:27.040
the warlord Duan Qirui captured Beijing, sent
Zhang Xun into exile, and forced Puyi to abdicate

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:28.149
all over again.

00:10:28.149 --> 00:10:30.810
This time, Puyi really would be the Last Emperor.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:38.810
The Boy and the Man
In early 1919, a man named Reginald Johnston

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:40.910
arrived outside the Forbidden City.

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:45.500
By now, China was three years deep into the
Warlord Era and the countryside was a wasteland.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.560
But Beijing was eerily calm.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:51.970
Ever since Duan Qirui had captured the city
and forced Puyi to abdicate for the second

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:54.760
time, no new warlords had tried to retake
the capital.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:58.530
It was during this lull that Duan’s government
had telephoned the British Foreign Office

00:10:58.530 --> 00:10:59.940
with an extraordinary request.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:02.070
They’d wanted an English language tutor.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:06.300
When asked why, they’d replied they wanted
lessons for their deposed emperor.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:11.089
And now here Reginald Johnston was, on an
official mission to teach Puyi English.

00:11:11.089 --> 00:11:15.490
Johnston’s first meeting with his new pupil
involved being led to a room deep in the Forbidden

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:18.639
City, and kowtowing three times to a thirteen
year old.

00:11:18.639 --> 00:11:22.490
Despite this, Johnston would later write that
he felt instant affection for the teenage

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:23.490
Puyi.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:27.310
Though it’s doubtful this affection would’ve
survived much contact with reality.

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:31.389
Unbeknownst to the teacher, Puyi was now a
fully-fledged sadist who had made flogging

00:11:31.389 --> 00:11:33.889
a random servant part of his daily routine.

00:11:33.889 --> 00:11:37.800
Over the following months, Johnston and Puyi
held daily classes.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.990
The more Johnston got to know Puyi, the more
he began to realize how much the boy who had

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:44.220
everything really had nothing.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:48.579
He discovered Puyi needed glasses, and that
he’d so far been refused because emperors

00:11:48.579 --> 00:11:50.250
weren’t expected to wear them.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:53.029
He discovered, too, that Puyi’s life was
spent in a gilded cage.

00:11:53.029 --> 00:11:55.720
That, even in this cage, servants were stealing
from him.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.050
Being a practical man, Johnston tried to help
his pupil.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:03.300
He got the boy glasses, arranged for him to
spend some time outside the palace.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:07.070
After a while, you might even say that the
man and the boy became friends.

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:10.329
It was this friendship that would save Puyi’s
life.

00:12:10.329 --> 00:12:13.130
Outside the walls of the Forbidden City, trouble
was brewing.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:18.829
In 1920, a coalition of warlords angry that
Japan had annexed South Manchuria drove Duan

00:12:18.829 --> 00:12:20.050
out of Beijing.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:24.570
It was the sixth change of regime Puyi had
survived since the 1912 revolution.

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:26.560
It was also nearly the last.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:32.040
In fall of 1924, two warlords, Zhang Zuolin
and Feng Yuxiang, forged an alliance to conquer

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:33.230
Beijing anew.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:37.230
As their armies swept into the city, Feng
made a bold pronouncement.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:42.199
He would not be bound by the 1912 treaty allowing
Puyi to continue living in the Forbidden City.

00:12:42.199 --> 00:12:46.209
Either the boy emperor left his imperial home,
or he would face the consequences.

00:12:46.209 --> 00:12:48.550
Not that Puyi was really a boy anymore.

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:50.790
Now aged 18, he even had a wife.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:54.649
In extremely awkward fashion, the imperial
court had given Puyi a stack of photos of

00:12:54.649 --> 00:12:58.699
girls in 1922 and told him to pick one to
be his bride.

00:12:58.699 --> 00:13:04.190
Piyu’s first choice, a girl named Wenxiu,
had turned out to be only 12, so instead he’d

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:08.880
married Empress Wanrong and taken the underage
Wenxiu as his consort.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.980
No, we don’t want to think too much about
that, either.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:15.960
As Feng’s army approached the palace, Reginald
Johnston desperately tried to find a foreign

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:17.810
embassy that would grant Puyi asylum.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:19.160
But there was no-one.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.790
Even Johnston’s own employers, the British,
refused.

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:25.200
Finally, at the last moment, Johnston had
his lightbulb moment.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.339
Why not ask the Japanese?

00:13:27.339 --> 00:13:33.170
On November 5, 1924, Puyi, Empress Wanrong,
and the consort Wenxiu all fled to the Japanese

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.170
embassy.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:37.010
About four months later, the Japanese offered
to move Puyi out of Beijing.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:39.050
We’ll send you somewhere safe, they said.

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:44.569
And so it was that, on February 23, 1925,
a disguised Puyi boarded a train bound for

00:13:44.569 --> 00:13:49.270
Tianjin, a Chinese city the Japanese had occupied
after winning the first Sino-Japanese War.

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:53.790
It was the start of a relationship between
the Last Emperor and Imperial Japan that would

00:13:53.790 --> 00:14:02.100
end in horrific bloodshed.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.610
The Last Emperor: Part II
For the next few years, Puyi lived a life

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:06.440
that can best be described as “dissolute”.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:11.740
In Japanese-controlled Tianjin, he and Empress
Wanrong reinvented themselves as a pair of

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:12.959
rich dilettantes.

00:14:12.959 --> 00:14:17.529
Puyi showered money on luxuries, pets and
society connections, using his faded imperial

00:14:17.529 --> 00:14:21.060
grandeur to gain entry to the city’s “whites
only” clubs.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:25.569
Wanrong, meanwhile, became addicted to opium,
spending her life in a listless daze.

00:14:25.569 --> 00:14:29.040
It’s likely the pair barely even noticed
as, across the border, Chiang Kai-shek’s

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:33.170
nationalist army finally defeated the warlords
and reunified China.

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.750
For his part, Puyi was far more concerned
with his impending divorce from Wenxiu.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:39.259
Remember, the underage consort?

00:14:39.259 --> 00:14:43.690
Well, she grew up, decided she could do better
than some rich sadist and left, even though

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:45.540
Puyi tried to bully her into staying.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.329
So, yay, well done Wenxiu.

00:14:47.329 --> 00:14:52.920
Yet it wouldn’t be quite true to say Puyi
spent his six years in Tianjin ignoring China.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:56.860
We know this because, in 1931, Puyi wrote
to the Japanese Minister for War, demanding

00:14:56.860 --> 00:14:58.829
help in restoring his empire.

00:14:58.829 --> 00:15:01.810
As luck would have it, the Japanese were in
need of an emperor.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:07.180
On September 18, 1931, the Japanese Imperial
Army had used the pretext of a terrorist attack

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.410
to annex the rest of Manchuria.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:11.260
They claimed they were restoring order.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:16.580
That Manchuria would now become the independent
state of Manchukuo, a place where Chinese,

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:19.769
Japanese, Koreans, Mongols, and Manchus could
live in harmony.

00:15:19.769 --> 00:15:24.069
In reality, of course, Manchukuo would be
a Japanese puppet state in which all other

00:15:24.069 --> 00:15:26.130
races were treated as inferior.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:29.100
But the Japanese didn’t want the world to
know this.

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:32.949
They needed a fig leaf of legitimacy to cover
their planned atrocities.

00:15:32.949 --> 00:15:38.889
A nominal ruler who was ethnically from Manchuria,
and could claim to be Manchukuo’s legitimate

00:15:38.889 --> 00:15:39.889
leader.

00:15:39.889 --> 00:15:43.070
In November, 1931, the Japanese wrote back
to Puyi.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:47.310
We can’t restore you to your old throne,
they told him, but hey, how about you come

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.560
rule this shiny new state we just created?

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:53.579
Ever the entitled fool, Puyi said yes.

00:15:53.579 --> 00:15:57.690
On March 9, 1932, Puyi became president of
Manchukuo.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:02.100
Outwardly, the Last Emperor appeared to have
everything he ever wanted: a state to rule,

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:03.980
a people who claimed to love him.

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:08.730
Only Puyi knew that, behind the scenes, he’d
signed secret documents transferring all power

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:12.109
in Manchukuo to the Japanese Imperial Army.

00:16:12.109 --> 00:16:14.540
.
In March of 1934, Puyi was elevated to Manchukuo’s

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:15.540
emperor.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:19.230
The Japanese reported the citizens of their
puppet state rejoiced.

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:20.800
But that seems unlikely.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:25.040
By 1934, it was clear just what a cruel regime
Puyi was leading.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:31.019
Manchukuo was a racist, authoritarian state
that persecuted non-Japanese citizens mercilessly.

00:16:31.019 --> 00:16:36.760
In 1932, for example, the Japanese responded
to an act of sabotage by rounding up 3,000

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.500
Chinese civilians and mass-executing them
all.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:42.250
But Puyi didn’t flinch at these atrocities.

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:44.500
He simply indulged in his own.

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:48.350
As Manchukuo’s emperor, Puyi was crueler
than he’d ever been in Beijing.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:52.290
He had servants beaten to death for the smallest
imagined infractions.

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:56.399
When he discovered Wanrong had been impregnated
by her Japanese lover, he had the newborn

00:16:56.399 --> 00:16:59.540
baby thrown alive into the palace boiler.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:03.440
While most of the blame for Manchukuo’s
cruelty can be placed at the feet of the Japanese,

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:05.900
Puyi was far from innocent.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:07.850
And things were about to get even worse.

00:17:07.850 --> 00:17:13.490
On July 7, 1937, a skirmish between Chinese
and Japanese forces at the Marco Polo Bridge

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:16.959
turned into a full-blown Japanese invasion
of China.

00:17:16.959 --> 00:17:19.699
The Second Sino-Japanese War had just begun.

00:17:19.699 --> 00:17:25.640
By the time it was over, both nations and
Manchukuo itself would lie in ruins.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:33.630
The Criminal and the Gardener
Of all the phony celebrations that have been

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:38.910
thrown by phony rulers over the years, none
can have been quite so phony as the one that

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:42.770
took place in Manchukuo in October, 1943.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:46.870
By that point, the Second Sino-Japanese War
had become just another front in WWII’s

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:48.280
epic punch up.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:51.920
Manchukuo had seen waves of show trials and
executions.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:55.280
Citizens had been turned into medical experiments
for Japanese doctors.

00:17:55.280 --> 00:18:01.600
Yet, that October day, Puyi still presided
over a “celebration” of ten years of Manchukuo

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:02.840
as an independent state.

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:07.610
It brings to mind Robespierre, throwing his
great feast in the middle of the Reign of

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:11.590
Terror, as thousands of Parisians were marched
to the guillotine.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:17.050
Yet even Puyi couldn’t have been as blind
as Robespierre to the fate that awaited him.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:21.120
He was the cruel, powerless emperor of a sadistic
failed state.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:23.750
There was only one way his last regime could
end.

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:28.350
In July, 1944, the first American planes began
bombing Manchukuo.

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:33.299
As cities fell in sheets of flame, Puyi clung
to his belief that - even if Japan was defeated

00:18:33.299 --> 00:18:37.309
- he would still be allowed to go on ruling
his ancestors’ homeland.

00:18:37.309 --> 00:18:42.450
By the time a year had passed, though, it
was clear Puyi’s hopes were so much ash.

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:46.830
On August 9, 1945 the USSR launched a ground
invasion of Manchukuo.

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:52.490
The arrival of the Red Army was like a cleansing
fire sweeping over Manchukuo.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:55.320
200,000 were killed in just two weeks.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:56.950
Not that Puyi was around to see it.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:01.140
Within a week of the first Soviet tanks entering
his territory, the emperor had been captured

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:02.309
by the Red Army.

00:19:02.309 --> 00:19:03.750
He would turn out to be the lucky one.

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:07.020
Empress Wanrong was taken alive by Chinese
partisans.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.390
She would starve to death in her jail cell
just one year later.

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:12.310
For Puyi, this was the end.

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:16.659
Chucked into a Siberian gulag, the Last Emperor
was now nothing but a mere war criminal.

00:19:16.659 --> 00:19:20.950
Although he lied through his teeth to save
his skin at the 1946 International Military

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:26.510
Tribunal for the Far East - the Japanese version
of the Nuremberg Trials - Puyi was done for.

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:27.600
And he knew it.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:29.980
The final act came just four years later.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:35.630
On October 1, 1949, Mao Zedong’s Communists
succeeded in taking control of mainland China.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:40.220
The new government in Beijing contacted the
Soviets, asking for their war criminal emperor

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:41.400
to be returned.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:42.799
The Soviets happily obliged.

00:19:42.799 --> 00:19:47.730
Shortly after, Puyi vanished for good into
the black hole of Mao’s internment camps.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:49.490
There was no public execution.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:52.270
No gunshot in a secluded courtyard.

00:19:52.270 --> 00:19:56.020
Just an emperor who stepped off a plane and
vanished.

00:19:56.020 --> 00:20:00.330
After three regimes and forty five years,
the tale of the Last Emperor was finally at

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:01.330
an end.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:02.380
...Or was it?

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:03.870
Fast forward a decade to 1960.

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:07.090
Imagine you are walking through Communist
Beijing.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:12.170
All around you, Chinese workers in overalls
ride bikes past concrete blocks.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:16.520
Up on the sides of buildings, Mao’s beaming
face stares down at you.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:20.299
It’s the most typical Communist scene you
can imagine.

00:20:20.299 --> 00:20:21.559
With one exception.

00:20:21.559 --> 00:20:23.299
See over there, by those flowerpots?

00:20:23.299 --> 00:20:26.440
See that old and withered gardener tending
to his plants?

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:31.610
He may look like just another ordinary peasant,
but that elderly gardener has some tall tales

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:32.690
to tell.

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:36.870
Tales about revolution and war, and about
growing up in a palace where everyone had

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:38.870
to do whatever he said.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:43.070
Tales about the three separate times that
he was China’s Last Emperor.

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:45.570
Of course, these are only stories.

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:50.460
You only have to look at the gardner to know
he’s a nobody, an old man who dreams of

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:51.580
being a somebody.

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:56.200
But there’s something in the way he sometimes
looks across the city, like he’s seeing

00:20:56.200 --> 00:21:00.070
a different Beijing, one that vanished decades
ago.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:03.900
Something about the way the Communist Party
sometimes calls on him to meet visiting dignitaries,

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:08.720
parades him around like he’s something more
than just a crazy old man.

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:12.570
Perhaps the strangest part is that the old
man never looks upset by this.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:16.760
Never looks as you’d imagine a former emperor
to look in such circumstances.

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:22.390
Instead, on these rare occasions, you can’t
help but notice how happy he appears.

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:27.620
Almost as if, after a lifetime locked in different
gilded cages, the elderly gardener who calls

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:46.299
himself Puyi is finally free.

